---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PlaceholderPlate from "../../components/PlaceholderPlate.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import { breadcrumbSchema } from "../../lib/schema";

const products = (await getCollection("products")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

// Natural branch pergola: timber species and assembly steps, shown under the drawings.
const pergolaSpecies = [
  { name: "Sweet chestnut", text: "Can be considered for natural-looking, rounded elements. Its heartwood is durable in outdoor environments; however, it should not be assumed that thin branches possess the same level of durability." },
  { name: "Black locust (Robinia)", text: "A strong choice for outdoor applications due to its durable heartwood. Its hard structure necessitates pre-drilling holes for connections." },
  { name: "Preservative-treated pine", text: "Suitable for use in load-bearing posts, beams, and rafters. The treatment class must be selected according to the specific outdoor conditions, including any potential ground contact." },
];
const pergolaAssembly = [
  "First, the load-bearing system is dimensioned. The cross-sections of posts, main beams, and rafters are determined based on the span, the weight of the branch covering, and local wind and snow conditions. For systems attached to a wall, the connections must reach the building’s structural framework; for freestanding pergolas, the design should include cross-bracing to limit lateral movement or incorporate engineered rigid joints.",
  "The branches are prepared prior to assembly. Pieces that are rotten, damaged by insects, or feature deep cracks in areas intended for connections should be discarded. Loose bark is removed; the branches are air-dried and sorted according to their diameter. In terms of project design, an arrangement where branches are installed individually—allowing for later replacement—offers advantages for maintenance.",
  "Branches should be positioned perpendicular to the rafters, ensuring they span across multiple supports. The spacing and orientation of the branches can be determined by testing a sample section, based on the angle of the sun and the desired density of the shade. The spacing between supports should be selected according to the diameter, curvature, and load-bearing capacity of the branches; there is no single standard measurement applicable to all installations.",
  "Fastening methods must be designed to prevent cracking and displacement caused by wind. Options include pre-drilling holes for exterior-grade screws or using mechanical clamps specifically designed for thinner branches. The diameter and length of the screws, their distance from the ends, and the total number of fasteners should be determined based on the actual cross-section of each branch. All fastening hardware must be compatible with any wood preservatives used; in coastal environments, the potential for corrosion must also be taken into account.",
  "Avoid joints that trap water. Details for the tops of beams, end-grain sections, and the contact points between branches and rafters should be designed to allow for proper drying. Whenever possible, wood preservative treatments should be applied prior to assembly. During maintenance, the structure should be inspected for any loosened connections or damaged branches. This covering provides shading.",
];

// Bespoke terracotta wall: brick production stages, shown side by side under the drawings.
const terracottaProcess = [
  "The prepared clay is typically placed into wooden molds by hand. Depending on the technique employed, the surface of the mold is either dusted with sand or wetted to ensure the clay can be easily removed. The clay is pressed into the mold to fill the corners, and any excess material on top is scraped away. Once removed from the mold, the unfired (green) brick is placed on a flat surface where it can retain its shape. Sanded molds tend to produce rougher surfaces, while wetted molds result in smoother ones.",
  "The bricks are dried slowly, protected from direct sunlight and strong air currents. Rapid or uneven moisture loss can lead to cracking or warping. The drying duration varies based on the properties of the clay, the thickness of the brick, and environmental conditions. When determining the dimensions of the molds, the shrinkage that occurs during drying and firing is taken into account.",
  "Once dried, the bricks are fired in a kiln under controlled conditions and subsequently cooled. The firing temperature and duration are determined by the composition of the clay used and the desired product specifications. The mineral structure of the clay, the atmosphere within the kiln, and the temperature distribution all influence the final color and strength of the bricks.",
  "The distinctive qualities of handmade bricks include surface markings, softened edges, and natural variations between individual pieces. However, these variations must remain within the dimensional and performance limits specified for the project. Following production, the bricks undergo checks for cracks, deformation, water absorption, and strength, ensuring that both their architectural character and technical adequacy are evaluated.",
];

const jsonLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Products Design", path: "/products" },
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Products Design"
  description={`Wall decor, canvas wall art, furniture, lighting and object design by ${SITE.name} — for home decor, living room design and office wall decor, developed alongside our interior commissions.`}
  path="/products"
  keywords={["wall decor", "duvar dekorasyonu", "canvas wall art", "kanvas tablo", "home decor", "ev dekorasyonu", "office decor", "ofis dekorasyonu", "office wall decor", "ofis duvar dekorasyonu", ...SITE.keywords]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:px-10 md:pt-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <p class="coord-mark">Products Design</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-4xl text-ink md:text-6xl">
        Objects that started on site.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg text-ink/75">
        Furniture, lighting and objects developed out of details, materials and offcuts from our
        architecture and interiors work — not designed separately from it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Natural branch pergola -->
  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 px-6 py-12 md:px-10 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl text-ink md:text-5xl">Natural Branch Pergola</h2>
      <!-- The installation photo is revealed on hover (or tap/focus on touch screens). -->
      <div class="group relative mt-8 cursor-pointer md:mt-10" tabindex="0">
        <img
          src="/images/products/natural-branch-pergola.jpg"
          alt="Pergola of natural timber branches laid over squared beams, casting striped shade across a white terrace with a potted olive tree, beside a view up into the branches against a blue sky"
          width="938"
          height="593"
          class="block h-auto w-full"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/products/natural-branch-pergola-installation.jpg"
          alt="Carpenter drilling a natural branch into place on a squared timber beam while laying the pergola roof"
          class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-500 group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus:opacity-100"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <p class="mt-8 max-w-3xl text-ink/80 md:mt-10">
        A natural branch pergola consists of a structural timber frame supporting closely spaced
        branches that form a permeable shading canopy. Primary beams and secondary rafters are
        sized according to the span, canopy weight, and site-specific wind loads, with connections
        designed to transfer forces to the supporting walls or posts. Selected branches are dried,
        debarked where required, and pre-drilled before being secured to the rafters with
        corrosion-resistant fixings to reduce splitting. Branch spacing and orientation are
        coordinated with solar exposure and the desired shading density, while allowing airflow
        and rainwater to pass through. Exterior-grade timber protection, moisture-resistant
        connection detailing, and access for periodic inspection and branch replacement support
        the durability of the installation.
      </p>
      <img
        src="/images/products/natural-branch-pergola-drawings.jpg"
        alt="Line drawings of the natural branch pergola: a perspective of the canopy over a shuttered terrace, a plan of the beams and branches, and a detail of branches fixed over a squared timber beam"
        width="1536"
        height="1024"
        class="mt-10 block h-auto w-full md:mt-14"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-3xl text-ink/80 md:mt-14">
        <p>
          In this system, the wood used for the load-bearing framework and the natural branches
          used for shading should be selected separately.
        </p>
        <h3 class="mt-10 font-display text-2xl text-ink md:text-3xl">Which tree species can be used?</h3>
        <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-stone">
          Material, appropriate use, and points to consider
        </p>
        <dl class="mt-4 divide-y divide-stone-light/70 border-y border-stone-light/70">
          {pergolaSpecies.map((species) => (
            <div class="py-4 md:grid md:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] md:gap-6">
              <dt class="font-display text-lg text-ink">{species.name}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1 md:mt-0">{species.text}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-6">
          The natural durability of sweet chestnut and black locust applies primarily to their
          heartwood. For thin branches—which often have a high proportion of bark and sapwood—the
          species name alone is not a sufficient indicator of durability.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h3 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl text-ink md:mt-16 md:text-3xl">How should assembly be carried out?</h3>
      <ol class="mt-8 grid gap-8 text-ink/80 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-5 lg:gap-8">
        {pergolaAssembly.map((step, i) => (
          <li class="border-t border-stone-light/70 pt-4">
            <span class="block font-display text-3xl text-ink">{i + 1}</span>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm">{step}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Bespoke terracotta wall -->
  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 px-6 py-12 md:px-10 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <h2 class="font-display text-3xl text-ink md:text-5xl">Bespoke Terracotta Wall</h2>
      <!-- The construction photo is revealed on hover (or tap/focus on touch screens). -->
      <div class="group relative mt-8 cursor-pointer md:mt-10" tabindex="0">
        <img
          src="/images/products/bespoke-terracotta-wall.jpg"
          alt="Offset terracotta brick screen filling a concrete frame above a covered walkway, beside tall terracotta lattice panels in a grey interior"
          width="1024"
          height="705"
          class="block h-auto w-full"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <img
          src="/images/products/bespoke-terracotta-wall-construction.jpg"
          alt="Bricklayers on scaffolding laying the offset terracotta brick screen inside the concrete frame, with pallets of bricks below"
          class="absolute inset-0 h-full w-full object-cover opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-500 group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus:opacity-100"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-4 text-ink/80 md:mt-10">
        <p>
          The architectural character of brick is shaped by how individual units are brought
          together. Project-specific brickwork unites the material’s texture, its bonding pattern,
          and the precision of its execution within a cohesive design vision. Every course, every
          mortar joint, and every void contributes to the facade as a whole.
        </p>
        <p>
          On this facade, an offset brick arrangement creates a permeable surface within a concrete
          frame. The openings between the bricks filter the light, while shifting shadows throughout
          the day lend depth to the surface. The warm tones of the terracotta and the natural
          variations in its texture reveal the material’s inherent character amidst a structured,
          geometric order.
        </p>
        <p>
          The translation of the design into reality relies on the careful attention of the
          craftsperson. The alignment of the courses, the continuity of the voids, and the
          consistency of the mortar joints ensure that the rhythm established in the drawings is
          preserved on-site. The resulting facade embodies a unique architectural expression—one
          shaped by the synergy between design precision and skilled craftsmanship.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        src="/images/products/terracotta-breeze-blocks.jpg"
        alt="Terracotta breeze blocks: stacked hollow square units against a white wall, a lattice screen in front of an outdoor sofa, and four quatrefoil blocks joined into a pattern"
        width="1024"
        height="420"
        class="mt-10 block h-auto w-full md:mt-14"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-4 text-ink/80 md:mt-10">
        <p>
          The production of handmade bricks consists of several stages: preparing the clay, molding
          it by hand, drying, and firing. The pressure applied by the craftsman, the surface of the
          mold, and the consistency of the clay all influence the texture and shape of each
          individual brick.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the initial stage, the clay is cleared of stones and coarse particles; it is then mixed
          with water and kneaded until it reaches a moldable consistency. Ensuring a homogeneous
          mixture allows the mold to be filled properly and helps minimize defects that might
          otherwise occur during the drying process.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        src="/images/products/bespoke-terracotta-wall-drawings.jpg"
        alt="Line drawings of the terracotta wall: a perspective of the offset brick screen in its concrete frame, an elevation, and a detail of hollow bricks threaded onto vertical steel rods"
        width="1672"
        height="941"
        class="mt-10 block h-auto w-full md:mt-14"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 text-sm text-ink/80 md:mt-10 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 lg:gap-8">
        {terracottaProcess.map((para) => (
          <p class="border-t border-stone-light/70 pt-4">{para}</p>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 px-6 py-12 md:px-10 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-10 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {
        products.map((product, i) => (
          <a href={`/products/${product.id}`} class="group block">
            {
              product.data.heroImage ? (
                <div class="w-full overflow-hidden">
                  <img
                    src={product.data.heroImage}
                    alt={product.data.summary}
                    class="h-auto w-full transition-transform duration-500 ease-out group-hover:scale-[1.4]"
                  />
                </div>
              ) : (
                <PlaceholderPlate
                  label={product.data.title}
                  seed={i + 60}
                  class="aspect-[4/5] w-full"
                />
              )
            }
            <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-stone">
              {product.data.category} · {product.data.year}
            </p>
            <h2 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-ink group-hover:text-patina">
              {product.data.title}
            </h2>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 bg-paper px-6 py-16 md:px-10 md:py-24">
    <div class="prose-content mx-auto max-w-3xl text-ink/85">
      <p class="coord-mark">Notes on memory</p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        An object is not merely something to be used. Over time, a person forms a relationship
        with the objects they touch, carry, keep, and use repeatedly in their daily life. A
        chair, table, pen, watch, or cup may initially be nothing more than a functional product.
        Yet, through use, it becomes scratched, worn, and altered in form, beginning to bear
        traces of its owner's life. Thus, the object ceases to be a mere physical tool and
        transforms into a part of lived experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        This relationship is one of the most significant—yet often overlooked—aspects of product
        design. A product's value lies not only in its form, ergonomics, or manufacturing
        quality, but also in the bond it can forge with a person over time. We do not use certain
        objects for years simply because they are technically superior; rather, those objects
        have aged alongside us, become part of specific moments, and embedded themselves in our
        personal histories.
      </p>
      <p>
        In contrast, an increasing number of products today are designed to be "memory-less."
        Instead of fostering a long-term relationship with the user, these products are built on
        rapid consumption, easy replacement, and constant renewal. Their surfaces are
        flawless—designed to conceal any signs of use—and when they break, they are replaced
        rather than repaired. A few years later, a new model of the same product hits the market,
        and the old one rapidly loses its value.
      </p>
      <p>
        "Memory-less" product design implies more than just the creation of short-lived goods; at
        a deeper level, it represents a design philosophy that precludes the product from
        evolving over time or being personalized by the user. Traces left by the user are viewed
        as flaws; scratches, discoloration, wear, and repairs are all deemed factors that
        diminish the product's value. Yet, these traces are the very layers that constitute the
        object's history. Scratches on the surface of an old wooden table, the altered shape of a
        leather armchair used for years, or the patina that develops on a metal object over
        time—all make the time the object has lived through visible. As each of these products
        undergoes physical change, it simultaneously keeps a record of its relationship with the
        user. In other words, the object carries memory within its very material.
      </p>
      <p>
        A product devoid of memory, however, seeks to remain as free of a past as possible. It is
        designed to be independent of the user, perpetually new, and interchangeable.
        Consequently, the user struggles to form a strong bond with the object. Even if the loss
        of a broken or worn-out product entails more than just the loss of a functional tool,
        replacing it with a new one is incredibly easy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perhaps one of the key questions for the future of product design is this: Can an object
        age alongside a human being? Can a designer envision not only the product's appearance on
        day one but also its state ten years down the line?
      </p>
      <p>
        Viewed in this light, product design is not merely about shaping an object; it is about
        designing the relationship that object will forge with time. A product with memory does
        not attempt to conceal its past. It embraces usage, undergoes transformation, and bears
        the traces of a person's life. For the true value of certain objects emerges not at the
        moment of their creation, but over the course of the time they live.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-content :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-content :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
